WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:10.189
Here is an example of an ethical question

2
00:00:10.199 --> 00:00:12.689
that might come up for cyber security professionals.

3
00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.569
An organization handling top secret and

4
00:00:15.579 --> 00:00:18.690
other sensitive information was hiring new employees

5
00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:24.100
at its facility. It used a retinal scanner to grant access to high security areas

6
00:00:24.309 --> 00:00:27.059
including where prospective employees were interviewed,

7
00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:28.670
retinal scanners,

8
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.829
unbeknownst to most people can not only

9
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.360
match blood vessels in an individual's retina,

10
00:00:33.439 --> 00:00:37.360
but they can also tell the difference between males and females further,

11
00:00:37.369 --> 00:00:39.439
they can tell whether a female is pregnant.

12
00:00:40.369 --> 00:00:44.209
The organization used this information gathered by its access control

13
00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:47.529
system to discriminate against female candidates for the positions.

14
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:48.950
It was seeking to fill

15
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.470
allowing this data to be accessed by those

16
00:00:51.479 --> 00:00:54.720
making hiring decisions was indisputably in violation of the

17
00:00:54.729 --> 00:00:58.310
code of ethics which states that information security professionals

18
00:00:58.319 --> 00:01:03.270
must act honorably honestly justly responsibly and legally.

19
00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:06.949
Here's another example,

20
00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:11.290
the security manager for an organization heard from a network administrator

21
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.459
who reported another user for violating

22
00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:15.769
the organization's acceptable use policy.

23
00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:19.129
When the security manager investigated the matter,

24
00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:21.410
he discovered several pertinent facts.

25
00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.260
The user did violate the policy.

26
00:01:25.069 --> 00:01:27.089
The violation was not a criminal matter.

27
00:01:27.839 --> 00:01:31.739
The network administrator had the it permissions to monitor the user.

28
00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:35.919
The network administrator was not tasked with monitoring the user

29
00:01:36.019 --> 00:01:39.690
nor was the administrator tasked with randomly monitoring all users.

30
00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:43.410
The network administrator would not say how the administrator

31
00:01:43.419 --> 00:01:46.150
came to learn that the user was violating policy

32
00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.309
in talking with colleagues of both people.

33
00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:51.379
It became clear that there was a personal

34
00:01:51.389 --> 00:01:54.059
conflict between the administrator and the user.

35
00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:57.529
In many jurisdictions.

36
00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.879
The organization can use any information regardless

37
00:02:00.889 --> 00:02:03.010
of source to make labor decisions.

38
00:02:03.239 --> 00:02:03.959
So yes,

39
00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:07.190
the organization could use this information against the user.

40
00:02:07.849 --> 00:02:11.210
The user violated the policy but did not break the law

41
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:14.300
depending on how egregious the infraction was.

42
00:02:14.419 --> 00:02:17.789
The organization may choose to punish the user for the violation

43
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.699
because the administrator would not explain why he was monitoring the user.

44
00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:26.990
It makes his actions suspect at best and nefarious at worst.

45
00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:31.339
The administrator violated the trust given to him by the organization.

46
00:02:31.669 --> 00:02:33.350
As an it professional,

47
00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.080
the administrator was expected to use authority and

48
00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:38.740
permissions in an adult and objective manner.

49
00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:42.309
This situation is almost certainly an example of the

50
00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:45.309
administrator using authority to settle a personal grievance.

51
00:02:45.929 --> 00:02:50.309
The administrator should be punished much more severely than the user firing.

52
00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.119
The administrator is not untoward.

53
00:02:52.399 --> 00:02:54.770
This person may have opened the organization up

54
00:02:54.779 --> 00:02:57.509
to a lawsuit for creating a hostile work environment

55
00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.869
which may have an impact risk that

56
00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.710
exceeds whatever policy violation the user committed,

57
00:03:03.809 --> 00:03:06.350
whether the administrator was terminated or not,

58
00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:09.779
his actions were in clear contradiction of the code of ethics.